WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:08.715
A very warm welcome to everyone
at Microsoft Research for

00:00:08.716 --> 00:00:14.175
the second workshop which is
on issues of AI and society.

00:00:14.176 --> 00:00:17.820
We've had a first workshop
in the past and part

00:00:17.821 --> 00:00:19.470
of the afternoon will be

00:00:19.471 --> 00:00:21.840
recapping what happened
the first time around.

00:00:21.841 --> 00:00:24.060
The major change
from the first time

00:00:24.061 --> 00:00:26.370
was that when we did
it the first time,

00:00:26.371 --> 00:00:31.220
it was primarily people
who are academics,

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:34.550
and research and practice,

00:00:34.551 --> 00:00:36.410
who were part of the workshop.

00:00:36.411 --> 00:00:40.580
The one thing we did
realize is that it makes

00:00:40.581 --> 00:00:42.530
a big difference to do
this with people who are

00:00:42.531 --> 00:00:45.750
actively in the social sector.

00:00:45.751 --> 00:00:48.480
As you've all probably realized,

00:00:48.481 --> 00:00:52.945
AI and society is
quite the buzzword these days.

00:00:52.946 --> 00:00:54.920
Even Resnick Anns latest film

00:00:54.921 --> 00:00:57.440
is about artificial intelligence.

00:00:57.441 --> 00:01:02.090
So, this is not just one
more event in that space.

00:01:02.091 --> 00:01:03.365
What we are trying to do is

00:01:03.366 --> 00:01:06.290
aggressively engage with
problems which are coming

00:01:06.291 --> 00:01:08.660
from the social sector and look

00:01:08.661 --> 00:01:11.275
at how technologies
can partner with that.

00:01:11.276 --> 00:01:15.630
So, the format of the day
is that the first half,

00:01:15.631 --> 00:01:20.225
which is Talkspace, is we'll
start off with the talk

00:01:20.226 --> 00:01:25.725
from the center director
and from the USC folks.

00:01:25.726 --> 00:01:31.460
USC is the driver with
MSR of this event.

00:01:31.461 --> 00:01:33.060
The second half of the day,

00:01:33.061 --> 00:01:37.130
we'll have non-profits and
NGOs talking about some of

00:01:37.131 --> 00:01:39.500
their problems that they'd like

00:01:39.501 --> 00:01:42.365
to apply various data
related solutions to.

00:01:42.366 --> 00:01:43.610
Following which, there will be

00:01:43.611 --> 00:01:46.490
breakout sessions in which
people will actually get

00:01:46.491 --> 00:01:48.425
together in groups
and talk about what

00:01:48.426 --> 00:01:51.285
technology can do to solve
some of those problems.

00:01:51.286 --> 00:01:52.600
So, in another sense,

00:01:52.601 --> 00:01:54.410
the second part of
the workshop is

00:01:54.411 --> 00:01:56.300
essentially a working workshop.

00:01:56.301 --> 00:01:59.160
So, if you want to
sneak away before that,

00:01:59.161 --> 00:02:01.700
the time is right after lunch.

00:02:01.701 --> 00:02:03.340
So, having said that,

00:02:03.341 --> 00:02:06.745
I'm going to go ahead and let
Sriram take it from here,

00:02:06.746 --> 00:02:08.840
the Director of MSR India and

00:02:08.841 --> 00:02:10.790
will introduce us to them, Raj.

00:02:10.791 --> 00:02:12.875
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much, Jogi.

00:02:12.876 --> 00:02:15.770
I want to thank all
colleagues from USC

00:02:15.771 --> 00:02:17.710
for making this trip all the

00:02:17.711 --> 00:02:20.170
way here and doing
this workshop together with us.

00:02:20.171 --> 00:02:22.490
I'm also very excited to

00:02:22.491 --> 00:02:25.520
welcome all the NGOs and
people from the social sector.

00:02:25.521 --> 00:02:28.400
As Jogi mentioned, we
had a previous event,

00:02:28.401 --> 00:02:31.640
but that one was mostly
academic in nature.

00:02:31.641 --> 00:02:33.290
I think I really want

00:02:33.291 --> 00:02:35.210
to welcome all of you
for taking the time

00:02:35.211 --> 00:02:36.830
to come here and participate in

00:02:36.831 --> 00:02:40.160
this conversation with all of us.

00:02:40.161 --> 00:02:42.240
This is a picture
of our first floor,

00:02:42.241 --> 00:02:44.000
if you haven't seen it
you should go upstairs.

00:02:44.001 --> 00:02:46.420
Absolutely beautiful office
we are blessed to have.

00:02:46.421 --> 00:02:51.430
I invite you to go upstairs
and check, our gig out.

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:54.380
So, the subject of my talk is

00:02:54.381 --> 00:02:57.020
to give some context
for who we are,

00:02:57.021 --> 00:03:00.385
and why we're doing
this. That's my goal.

00:03:00.386 --> 00:03:01.820
So, that you have a sense for

00:03:01.821 --> 00:03:03.415
why we're doing what we're doing.

00:03:03.416 --> 00:03:04.910
To that end I shared, I wanted to

00:03:04.911 --> 00:03:06.800
first introduce
Microsoft Research.

00:03:06.801 --> 00:03:09.500
Microsoft Research was set up

00:03:09.501 --> 00:03:12.075
in 1991 and we are
a global organization.

00:03:12.076 --> 00:03:14.120
We have four labs in
the United States,

00:03:14.121 --> 00:03:16.160
one in the West Coast, and
three in the East Coast,

00:03:16.161 --> 00:03:17.535
we have one in UK,

00:03:17.536 --> 00:03:18.785
one here in Bangalore,

00:03:18.786 --> 00:03:20.885
and one in China in Beijing.

00:03:20.886 --> 00:03:22.705
We are more than a
thousand people;

00:03:22.706 --> 00:03:24.765
850 PhDs,

00:03:24.766 --> 00:03:26.680
which Rick Rashid computes it

00:03:26.681 --> 00:03:28.520
as n Computer
Science departments.

00:03:28.521 --> 00:03:31.540
I guess it is 850 divided by
50, whatever that number is.

00:03:31.541 --> 00:03:35.295
That many Computer Science
departments, and engineers,

00:03:35.296 --> 00:03:38.325
and development horsepower,

00:03:38.326 --> 00:03:41.110
to take those ideas actually
and transition them.

00:03:41.111 --> 00:03:43.590
This is our mission and

00:03:43.591 --> 00:03:45.735
I think Rick Rashid was
a big Star Trek fan.

00:03:45.736 --> 00:03:49.850
So, I think this mission is
somewhat modeled around I

00:03:49.851 --> 00:03:54.530
guess this is to explore
new worlds, civilizations.

00:03:54.531 --> 00:03:56.630
I think the last one is
to boldly go nowhere

00:03:56.631 --> 00:03:59.635
no man has gone
before and whatever.

00:03:59.636 --> 00:04:01.020
So, you can see.

00:04:01.021 --> 00:04:04.970
But it's interesting and
inspiring that the mission is

00:04:04.971 --> 00:04:06.680
written in such a way that it

00:04:06.681 --> 00:04:09.560
doesn't talk about
Microsoft's current products.

00:04:09.561 --> 00:04:11.010
It gives us Microsoft Research,

00:04:11.011 --> 00:04:15.800
the ability to have as
its first and foremost goal,

00:04:15.801 --> 00:04:17.480
to expand the state
of the art which

00:04:17.481 --> 00:04:21.220
really drives the culture
of this organization.

00:04:21.610 --> 00:04:24.445
This is us, this is
Microsoft Research India.

00:04:24.446 --> 00:04:26.520
We were set up in 2005,

00:04:26.521 --> 00:04:28.775
so we just became a teenager.

00:04:28.776 --> 00:04:32.845
I think we are now 13 years old
and this is all of us.

00:04:32.846 --> 00:04:35.310
You can fit us all
in one picture.

00:04:35.311 --> 00:04:38.805
I always like to brag
about my colleagues.

00:04:38.806 --> 00:04:40.680
So, these are awards

00:04:40.681 --> 00:04:43.395
that my colleagues
have won recently.

00:04:43.396 --> 00:04:46.860
Bill, this was
a year before last,

00:04:46.861 --> 00:04:49.035
he won the McCarthy prize.

00:04:49.036 --> 00:04:52.720
We had three ACM
Fellows recently.

00:04:52.721 --> 00:04:56.985
Venkat also won the Shanti
Swarup Bhatnagar Prize,

00:04:56.986 --> 00:04:59.330
which is one of
the highest awards

00:04:59.331 --> 00:05:02.040
that the Indian government
offers to scientific research.

00:05:02.041 --> 00:05:03.350
It is the first time
they've given it to

00:05:03.351 --> 00:05:05.180
someone who's not in

00:05:05.181 --> 00:05:09.110
an IAD or a DRDO or
government organization.

00:05:09.111 --> 00:05:11.690
They gave it to someone working
in a multinational Lab,

00:05:11.691 --> 00:05:13.925
which is a real testament to

00:05:13.926 --> 00:05:17.895
how people value and
respect what we do.

00:05:17.896 --> 00:05:21.995
Indrani recently won
the SGCHi Social Impact Award.

00:05:21.996 --> 00:05:24.310
That's the sense for the
colleagues that we have.

00:05:24.311 --> 00:05:26.910
This is our younger crowd

00:05:26.911 --> 00:05:29.100
in addition to
the full-time people.

00:05:29.101 --> 00:05:30.200
We have a program called

00:05:30.201 --> 00:05:34.505
Research Fellow Program where
we recruit undergraduates,

00:05:34.506 --> 00:05:35.690
people who have
finished bachelor's and

00:05:35.691 --> 00:05:39.910
master's and we recruit
them as research trainees.

00:05:39.911 --> 00:05:41.555
They spend one to
two years with us.

00:05:41.556 --> 00:05:43.835
We currently have
40 Research Fellows,

00:05:43.836 --> 00:05:45.680
I think we run the risk of

00:05:45.681 --> 00:05:47.720
them exceeding the number

00:05:47.721 --> 00:05:49.395
of full-time employees
I think sometime soon.

00:05:49.396 --> 00:05:51.200
We actually started with
five Research Fellows,

00:05:51.201 --> 00:05:52.390
now we have 40.

00:05:52.391 --> 00:05:53.840
For those 40 positions,

00:05:53.841 --> 00:05:56.470
we get more than a
thousand applications.

00:05:56.471 --> 00:05:59.250
The best from all the IADs,
and BITS Pilani's,

00:05:59.251 --> 00:06:00.995
and the TUPLETE's apply there

00:06:00.996 --> 00:06:03.420
and they bring so much energy,

00:06:03.421 --> 00:06:05.980
much like students
bring in academia.

00:06:05.981 --> 00:06:08.980
We have 40 of them
currently here.

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:13.190
So, one thing that happens
is that a lot of them go

00:06:13.191 --> 00:06:16.200
do PhDs after they
finish their time here.

00:06:16.201 --> 00:06:18.675
They could apply to
UCS, or in Berkeley,

00:06:18.676 --> 00:06:20.315
they go to US universities,

00:06:20.316 --> 00:06:21.890
European universities, but some

00:06:21.891 --> 00:06:23.540
of them decide not
to do research.

00:06:23.541 --> 00:06:24.650
The ones that decide not to do

00:06:24.651 --> 00:06:26.180
research tend to do better.

00:06:26.181 --> 00:06:28.420
So, Ola was started
by one of them.

00:06:28.421 --> 00:06:31.915
By any measure of imagination,
I think he's doing well.

00:06:31.916 --> 00:06:34.320
Capillary was started
by Krishna Mehra,

00:06:34.321 --> 00:06:37.160
who was an Arif with me and
these are other companies

00:06:37.161 --> 00:06:40.350
that were started out of
staff that led in the lab.

00:06:40.351 --> 00:06:41.825
That gives you a sense for how

00:06:41.826 --> 00:06:45.465
entrepreneurial the lab is.

00:06:45.466 --> 00:06:48.045
The large measure it is
due to the young people,

00:06:48.046 --> 00:06:51.440
the new talent that
we get every year.

00:06:51.640 --> 00:06:54.830
This describes the work
that we do in this lab,

00:06:54.831 --> 00:06:58.635
our work is organized
on four major areas.

00:06:58.636 --> 00:07:00.560
We do a lot of work in theory

00:07:00.561 --> 00:07:02.470
and foundations of
computer science,

00:07:02.471 --> 00:07:04.955
mathematics behind
machine learning,

00:07:04.956 --> 00:07:08.770
we have a very strong group
that does core

00:07:08.771 --> 00:07:11.340
or machine learning and
artificial intelligence,

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:14.675
algorithms, tools,

00:07:14.676 --> 00:07:18.075
implementations, natural
language processing.

00:07:18.076 --> 00:07:21.710
So, we are very well represented
in those areas as well.

00:07:21.711 --> 00:07:23.930
We have a very strong
group and systems

00:07:23.931 --> 00:07:25.914
which is the foundations
and infrastructure,

00:07:25.915 --> 00:07:27.870
based on which
everything is built.

00:07:27.871 --> 00:07:30.050
There is a very unique
group in this lab

00:07:30.051 --> 00:07:32.255
called technologies
are emerging markets,

00:07:32.256 --> 00:07:35.570
which studies the role
of technology in

00:07:35.571 --> 00:07:37.610
socioeconomic development and it

00:07:37.611 --> 00:07:40.230
is most relevant to
today's meeting.

00:07:40.231 --> 00:07:42.530
This is an interdisciplinary
group that

00:07:42.531 --> 00:07:44.510
contains both computer
scientists as well as

00:07:44.511 --> 00:07:47.060
social scientists and
a lot of the work that I

00:07:47.061 --> 00:07:50.190
will talk about today
is from this group.

00:07:50.191 --> 00:07:52.310
But increasingly, the area

00:07:52.311 --> 00:07:54.860
of technology for societal
good is becoming very

00:07:54.861 --> 00:07:57.530
interdisciplinary and
people with background in

00:07:57.531 --> 00:07:58.970
systems and machine learning are

00:07:58.971 --> 00:08:00.670
also doing work in these areas.

00:08:00.671 --> 00:08:02.415
So, I would broadly
represent work

00:08:02.416 --> 00:08:04.900
from the entire lab in this area.

00:08:04.901 --> 00:08:09.860
So, actually, I also wanted
to mention that as a lab,

00:08:09.861 --> 00:08:13.910
we think about our work having
impact along three axis,

00:08:13.911 --> 00:08:16.880
one is in science
advancing the state of art

00:08:16.881 --> 00:08:18.760
in anything and everything

00:08:18.761 --> 00:08:20.800
that we do, the second
one is company.

00:08:20.801 --> 00:08:22.490
There's a lot of stuff that we do

00:08:22.491 --> 00:08:24.350
that gets transitioned into

00:08:24.351 --> 00:08:25.490
either existing products or

00:08:25.491 --> 00:08:28.700
inspires new products and
services for our company.

00:08:28.701 --> 00:08:32.060
We explicitly acknowledge
in helping society

00:08:32.061 --> 00:08:35.320
as an important part of the axis
of impact that we have.

00:08:35.321 --> 00:08:37.175
So, for the people
that are here from

00:08:37.176 --> 00:08:39.635
NGOs and social enterprises,

00:08:39.636 --> 00:08:40.820
one thing that I should make very

00:08:40.821 --> 00:08:42.260
clear is that the work that

00:08:42.261 --> 00:08:45.165
we do in this space
is not CSR activity.

00:08:45.166 --> 00:08:47.330
Our company has a CSR activity.

00:08:47.331 --> 00:08:49.850
Then, there is a law in
India that two percent

00:08:49.851 --> 00:08:52.805
of every company's revenue
is given and we do that too,

00:08:52.806 --> 00:08:54.775
but this workshop
is not about that.

00:08:54.776 --> 00:08:57.130
This workshop is about us

00:08:57.131 --> 00:09:00.630
engaging as Scientists and
Technologists with you,

00:09:00.631 --> 00:09:04.860
to help you solve problems
in collaboration with us.

00:09:04.861 --> 00:09:06.615
That's what this
workshop is about.

00:09:06.616 --> 00:09:09.695
The other interesting thing
that has happened is that

00:09:09.696 --> 00:09:12.955
I joined MSR about 19 years ago,

00:09:12.956 --> 00:09:14.535
and when I joined MSR,

00:09:14.536 --> 00:09:17.390
Microsoft was a Windows
and Office company.

00:09:17.391 --> 00:09:21.065
So, a lot of the
work that we did in

00:09:21.066 --> 00:09:22.720
impacting society was largely

00:09:22.721 --> 00:09:24.175
of no relevance to the company.

00:09:24.176 --> 00:09:26.080
It was not relevant to
the company's products,

00:09:26.081 --> 00:09:30.720
but if I now look at
our cloud and AI offerings,

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:33.780
is very integral to what
our accompany does.

00:09:33.781 --> 00:09:35.220
We do want to engage
with government,

00:09:35.221 --> 00:09:37.610
we want to engage with
large communities,

00:09:37.611 --> 00:09:39.825
people understand what problems
they want to solve.

00:09:39.826 --> 00:09:41.750
So, it's very much something that

00:09:41.751 --> 00:09:42.950
our company commercially is

00:09:42.951 --> 00:09:44.220
actually interested in as well.

00:09:44.221 --> 00:09:45.665
You'll see examples of that

00:09:45.666 --> 00:09:48.300
in the slides that
I'll show you next.

00:09:49.050 --> 00:09:52.335
So, here I have some examples

00:09:52.336 --> 00:09:53.880
of the kinds of things

00:09:53.881 --> 00:09:55.500
that this lab has
done in the past.

00:09:55.501 --> 00:09:57.390
So, I will start with
mature projects,

00:09:57.391 --> 00:10:00.200
projects that have
had the benefit

00:10:00.201 --> 00:10:03.230
of time and see where
they have gone now.

00:10:03.231 --> 00:10:05.415
The first one on the far
left is a project called

00:10:05.416 --> 00:10:08.775
IVR Junction that was
started by Will Thies.

00:10:08.776 --> 00:10:11.515
This is a interactive
voice response system

00:10:11.516 --> 00:10:13.465
so that people

00:10:13.466 --> 00:10:19.050
in remote and communities
where people have

00:10:19.051 --> 00:10:21.030
access to a feature phone
but perhaps don't

00:10:21.031 --> 00:10:23.340
have the literacy skills
or they don't have

00:10:23.341 --> 00:10:26.460
a smart phone to
engage on social media

00:10:26.461 --> 00:10:30.000
like Facebook or
LinkedIn or Twitter.

00:10:30.001 --> 00:10:31.220
But nevertheless, I think they

00:10:31.221 --> 00:10:32.350
have very interesting
things to say,

00:10:32.351 --> 00:10:34.290
they have issues like most of us.

00:10:34.291 --> 00:10:36.910
Just like we get
upset when we get

00:10:36.911 --> 00:10:39.200
a defective product and
we can flame on Facebook,

00:10:39.201 --> 00:10:41.480
they would like to do
the same thing as well.

00:10:41.481 --> 00:10:45.320
So, IVR Junction was a piece of

00:10:45.321 --> 00:10:46.330
infrastructure that was developed

00:10:46.331 --> 00:10:48.950
by Bill and his colleagues,

00:10:48.951 --> 00:10:52.224
and then it was adopted
first by CGNet-Swara,

00:10:52.225 --> 00:10:55.120
which is an NGO that
works in Gondwana

00:10:55.121 --> 00:11:00.110
and several other
NGOs in the world,

00:11:00.111 --> 00:11:04.030
even I know that Somali land
has actually adopted it.

00:11:04.031 --> 00:11:06.160
You could go to the IVR
Junction web page and you will

00:11:06.161 --> 00:11:08.330
see a huge list of
customers that are using

00:11:08.331 --> 00:11:14.420
it to have these people
raise their issues,

00:11:14.421 --> 00:11:18.970
schools where teachers
have not been paid,

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:23.155
their grievance has
been addressed.

00:11:23.156 --> 00:11:26.660
There was a case of
a hospital which was only on

00:11:26.661 --> 00:11:28.090
paper but there was
actually no hospital at

00:11:28.091 --> 00:11:29.610
all. So, that has
been discovered.

00:11:29.611 --> 00:11:31.630
So, it's very powerful and

00:11:31.631 --> 00:11:34.575
empowering to people who
didn't have a voice before.

00:11:34.576 --> 00:11:37.810
MEC is an online
learning experiment

00:11:37.811 --> 00:11:39.105
that we did in this lab.

00:11:39.106 --> 00:11:40.150
Which is we actually study

00:11:40.151 --> 00:11:41.210
something called
blended learning,

00:11:41.211 --> 00:11:42.825
where we combine online learning

00:11:42.826 --> 00:11:44.905
together with
in-classroom education.

00:11:44.906 --> 00:11:46.745
That was actually picked up by

00:11:46.746 --> 00:11:48.855
our development center
in Hyderabad.

00:11:48.856 --> 00:11:51.670
It has now evolved
into Project Sangam,

00:11:51.671 --> 00:11:53.670
where the online learning system

00:11:53.671 --> 00:11:55.550
is also being integrated
with LinkedIn,

00:11:55.551 --> 00:11:58.240
and it is being offered as
a service for skilling and

00:11:58.241 --> 00:12:02.510
job training for
low income labor.

00:12:02.511 --> 00:12:05.720
So, it's very interesting.

00:12:05.810 --> 00:12:08.060
Something that we didn't
anticipate that would

00:12:08.061 --> 00:12:10.050
happen when we originally
started this project.

00:12:10.051 --> 00:12:12.120
Digital Green is a video

00:12:12.121 --> 00:12:13.440
enabled agricultural
extension which

00:12:13.441 --> 00:12:14.340
was started as a project in

00:12:14.341 --> 00:12:16.540
his lab and now it runs
in tens of thousands of

00:12:16.541 --> 00:12:19.830
villages both in
India and in Africa.

00:12:19.831 --> 00:12:24.280
99DOTS, and I think Emmy is here,

00:12:24.281 --> 00:12:26.360
so she will give an update
on 99DOTS later.

00:12:26.361 --> 00:12:29.285
It's an innovative
mHealth platform

00:12:29.286 --> 00:12:31.635
for tuberculosis
medication adherence.

00:12:31.636 --> 00:12:33.310
This is a project that
was started here,

00:12:33.311 --> 00:12:36.775
and now we expanded off into
a startup called Everwell,

00:12:36.776 --> 00:12:38.570
Andrew Cross is the CEO of

00:12:38.571 --> 00:12:41.145
Everwell and it's
right down the street.

00:12:41.146 --> 00:12:42.360
So, I think if you have
time, you could go

00:12:42.361 --> 00:12:45.720
visit Everwell as well.

00:12:45.910 --> 00:12:48.320
So, I wanted to give
you a sense for

00:12:48.321 --> 00:12:50.510
the current projects that
are going on in this lab,

00:12:50.511 --> 00:12:54.060
and my objective behind
doing this is so that you

00:12:54.061 --> 00:12:55.900
get a sense for the kind of

00:12:55.901 --> 00:12:58.470
problems that our staff
is interested in.

00:12:58.471 --> 00:13:01.590
Because the goal of
this workshop is to

00:13:01.591 --> 00:13:03.770
open collaborative
conversations between

00:13:03.771 --> 00:13:05.215
our staff and all of you.

00:13:05.216 --> 00:13:08.070
So, I hope that these things
give you the sense

00:13:08.071 --> 00:13:11.555
for the kind of things that
our staff was interested in.

00:13:11.556 --> 00:13:14.210
So, let me go from left to right.

00:13:14.211 --> 00:13:15.935
So, the current work in the lab,

00:13:15.936 --> 00:13:18.585
there is a lot of
interest in work.

00:13:18.586 --> 00:13:20.615
So, Karya for example,

00:13:20.616 --> 00:13:23.870
is a project where we are

00:13:23.871 --> 00:13:28.280
targeting Mechanical Turk like

00:13:28.281 --> 00:13:32.595
crowd-work for digital labor,

00:13:32.596 --> 00:13:37.180
for transcription,
for labeling text,

00:13:37.181 --> 00:13:40.060
for labeling date of speech,

00:13:40.061 --> 00:13:44.010
so that automated
NLP can actually

00:13:44.011 --> 00:13:45.520
be enabled for languages for

00:13:45.521 --> 00:13:47.820
which we don't have
any training data.

00:13:47.821 --> 00:13:50.445
We are working with
government to see

00:13:50.446 --> 00:13:53.615
if the people who this crowd
work can actually be paid.

00:13:53.616 --> 00:13:55.500
In a lot of cases, the government
is actually willing to

00:13:55.501 --> 00:13:58.375
pay for such work.

00:13:58.376 --> 00:14:02.515
So, Karya is such a project.

00:14:02.516 --> 00:14:04.015
Avatar is very interesting,

00:14:04.016 --> 00:14:05.650
it actually uses again,

00:14:05.651 --> 00:14:07.505
crowd work to do verification.

00:14:07.506 --> 00:14:09.660
Say you are an NGO and
you have donated money,

00:14:09.661 --> 00:14:11.110
and somebody has built a toilet.

00:14:11.111 --> 00:14:13.560
How do you know that the toilet
has been actually built?

00:14:13.561 --> 00:14:16.380
So, this one uses
crowd sourcing to have

00:14:16.381 --> 00:14:19.030
someone go there
stream a video and

00:14:19.031 --> 00:14:21.320
then verify that a toilet
has been built and

00:14:21.321 --> 00:14:23.900
we could pay and make

00:14:23.901 --> 00:14:25.110
small micro payment and we

00:14:25.111 --> 00:14:26.460
could do this verification
any number of

00:14:26.461 --> 00:14:29.015
times so that if there's
something wrong,

00:14:29.016 --> 00:14:30.600
we will come to know
about it, right?

00:14:30.601 --> 00:14:33.120
So, that's another one.

00:14:34.170 --> 00:14:36.970
Kaizala is a very
interesting platform that is

00:14:36.971 --> 00:14:39.040
actually being built by
our development center.

00:14:39.041 --> 00:14:41.695
It is a little bit like
WhatsApp but for enterprise.

00:14:41.696 --> 00:14:43.905
So, it has a WhatsApp
like interface,

00:14:43.906 --> 00:14:45.455
a chat interface for

00:14:45.456 --> 00:14:48.075
the client but it also
has a server-side.

00:14:48.076 --> 00:14:50.635
Where you can now
write server code

00:14:50.636 --> 00:14:53.690
to manage all the
information and so on.

00:14:53.691 --> 00:14:56.005
It has enterprise component of

00:14:56.006 --> 00:14:59.060
chat as well and Jackie O'Neill
has been working

00:14:59.061 --> 00:15:01.155
with that group to understand

00:15:01.156 --> 00:15:03.500
the social implications of

00:15:03.501 --> 00:15:05.250
how such a product
gets used by people.

00:15:05.251 --> 00:15:07.000
The customers for
this turns out to be

00:15:07.001 --> 00:15:09.350
Andhra Pradesh government SERP,

00:15:09.351 --> 00:15:10.695
which turns out to be Society

00:15:10.696 --> 00:15:12.900
for Elimination of Rural Poverty.

00:15:12.901 --> 00:15:14.830
So, the customers of
something like this

00:15:14.831 --> 00:15:16.945
turn out to be
field workers who do

00:15:16.946 --> 00:15:18.980
society work and we
engage with them to

00:15:18.981 --> 00:15:22.470
understand both technical
and non-technical aspects

00:15:22.471 --> 00:15:24.325
of how such a product
should be built.

00:15:24.326 --> 00:15:26.150
BlendNet is another very

00:15:26.151 --> 00:15:27.710
interesting project
where we are trying to

00:15:27.711 --> 00:15:31.915
use peer to peer connectivity

00:15:31.916 --> 00:15:35.315
to transfer bulk data
between mobiles.

00:15:35.316 --> 00:15:37.910
So, thanks to Reliance Jio,

00:15:37.911 --> 00:15:40.265
2G, 3G connectivity is
very prevalent in India.

00:15:40.266 --> 00:15:42.695
But it is still very hard
to download or upload

00:15:42.696 --> 00:15:46.225
bulk media like video is
actually always hard to do.

00:15:46.226 --> 00:15:48.800
So, BlendNet uses peer
to peer connectivity

00:15:48.801 --> 00:15:51.630
for bulk data but still

00:15:51.631 --> 00:15:54.340
uses Azure as
a network in order to

00:15:54.341 --> 00:15:55.560
make sure that you can do peer to

00:15:55.561 --> 00:15:57.195
peer without knowing
the other person.

00:15:57.196 --> 00:15:58.290
So, you don't have to know who

00:15:58.291 --> 00:15:59.700
the other person is
to get the video.

00:15:59.701 --> 00:16:00.870
Azure knows who has

00:16:00.871 --> 00:16:03.170
what video and it'll actually
stands for that for you.

00:16:03.171 --> 00:16:05.830
Learn2Earn is
a very interesting system

00:16:05.831 --> 00:16:09.890
where it uses micro-payments and

00:16:09.891 --> 00:16:13.945
incentives for people to

00:16:13.946 --> 00:16:16.560
learn information and
transmit it to others.

00:16:16.561 --> 00:16:18.410
So, it is actually a platform for

00:16:18.411 --> 00:16:22.110
widely spreading
information dissemination.

00:16:22.850 --> 00:16:25.690
There's more, I'm not
going go through more

00:16:25.691 --> 00:16:27.710
of these and we already
mentioned 99DOTS,

00:16:27.711 --> 00:16:29.060
we have Amit Sharma
who is working

00:16:29.061 --> 00:16:31.310
on technology for
mental health issues.

00:16:31.311 --> 00:16:33.070
This is for financial inclusion.

00:16:33.071 --> 00:16:34.590
We have a lot of work
that is going on

00:16:34.591 --> 00:16:37.905
around technology
for accessibility.

00:16:37.906 --> 00:16:40.310
That's a core area
of the lab now.

00:16:40.311 --> 00:16:42.685
I want to show you a few more.

00:16:42.686 --> 00:16:44.590
Natural language processing is

00:16:44.591 --> 00:16:46.480
also very important area
in this lab.

00:16:46.481 --> 00:16:49.080
Since this workshop is
about AI and societal good,

00:16:49.081 --> 00:16:51.590
I should call out
specifically that NLP has

00:16:51.591 --> 00:16:55.140
a huge promise for
bringing people

00:16:55.141 --> 00:16:58.290
who have very low
textual literacy

00:16:58.291 --> 00:17:01.250
and make the benefits

00:17:01.251 --> 00:17:04.280
of digital technology
accessible to this population,

00:17:04.281 --> 00:17:06.265
which is a huge
population in India.

00:17:06.266 --> 00:17:08.260
AI, I think has a huge amount of

00:17:08.261 --> 00:17:11.625
promise in inclusion for
serving these communities,

00:17:11.626 --> 00:17:14.680
but there are significant
technical challenges because of

00:17:14.681 --> 00:17:16.040
simply the absence of

00:17:16.041 --> 00:17:17.710
annotated data for lots

00:17:17.711 --> 00:17:19.385
and lots of these kinds
of languages.

00:17:19.386 --> 00:17:22.150
So, that's actually a topic
that we actively work on.

00:17:22.151 --> 00:17:24.245
Also I see Caliga and [inaudible]
back in the audience,

00:17:24.246 --> 00:17:26.055
they study low-resource languages

00:17:26.056 --> 00:17:27.890
and they have an active
engagement going on with

00:17:27.891 --> 00:17:33.155
CGNet-Swara to make an AI
and NLP work for Gondi,

00:17:33.156 --> 00:17:34.250
which is a tribal language with

00:17:34.251 --> 00:17:36.685
absolutely no annotations today.

00:17:36.686 --> 00:17:39.420
IoT is another area
where we do a lot of

00:17:39.421 --> 00:17:42.825
work and we have work
in Internet of Things,

00:17:42.826 --> 00:17:46.525
sensors for agriculture,
for safety.

00:17:46.526 --> 00:17:48.830
How do you use
sensors to make sure

00:17:48.831 --> 00:17:51.330
that we can monitor drivers and

00:17:51.331 --> 00:17:56.055
help them have safe driving
behavior and so on.

00:17:56.056 --> 00:17:57.690
If you're here you
might actually also

00:17:57.691 --> 00:17:59.375
get a demo of
an interactive game.

00:17:59.376 --> 00:18:03.460
I think Shir upstairs
can give you a demo.

00:18:03.461 --> 00:18:05.540
These projects are projects where

00:18:05.541 --> 00:18:08.190
we are using blockchain,

00:18:08.191 --> 00:18:09.770
those kinds of technologies for

00:18:09.771 --> 00:18:12.120
decentralized identity for making

00:18:12.121 --> 00:18:13.160
sure that there is trust and

00:18:13.161 --> 00:18:14.690
transparency in what people do.

00:18:14.691 --> 00:18:16.490
Many of the NGOs
might be interested

00:18:16.491 --> 00:18:18.090
in technologies that can ensure

00:18:18.091 --> 00:18:19.445
that you can actually track

00:18:19.446 --> 00:18:21.490
where your funds are
going and so on.

00:18:21.491 --> 00:18:23.850
So, we have technologies
that are based on blockchain

00:18:23.851 --> 00:18:26.830
to do those kinds of activities.

00:18:26.831 --> 00:18:28.770
Hopefully, this gives
you a sense for the kind

00:18:28.771 --> 00:18:30.915
of work that this lab does.

00:18:30.916 --> 00:18:32.320
Please keep this in mind,

00:18:32.321 --> 00:18:33.690
because I'm not saying
actually we will engage

00:18:33.691 --> 00:18:35.030
with you only on these topics but

00:18:35.031 --> 00:18:36.435
hopefully this gives you a sense

00:18:36.436 --> 00:18:38.730
for where people are coming from.

00:18:38.731 --> 00:18:42.500
Okay? So, I wanted to
show this slide as

00:18:42.501 --> 00:18:44.150
a meta slide of what we have

00:18:44.151 --> 00:18:46.755
learned over the years
doing all these projects.

00:18:46.756 --> 00:18:49.430
Be it health, be it
education, and so on.

00:18:49.431 --> 00:18:53.920
So, one of the things

00:18:53.921 --> 00:18:55.175
about this lab is that

00:18:55.176 --> 00:18:58.895
I would say in addition
to writing papers,

00:18:58.896 --> 00:19:01.425
we actually deploy
what we've built here.

00:19:01.426 --> 00:19:02.630
In fact actually, we write

00:19:02.631 --> 00:19:04.105
a paper when we have
something to say,

00:19:04.106 --> 00:19:06.640
and a paper is usually
a byproduct of what we do

00:19:06.641 --> 00:19:08.260
rather than something that we do

00:19:08.261 --> 00:19:10.005
because there's
a conference deadline.

00:19:10.006 --> 00:19:12.690
I mentioned this not to
disparage economics but to

00:19:12.691 --> 00:19:15.345
reflect on my own experience
as a student, right?

00:19:15.346 --> 00:19:16.750
Because as a student, you have
to do that because that's

00:19:16.751 --> 00:19:18.375
only way you can graduate,

00:19:18.376 --> 00:19:19.460
and as faculty
then, we have to do

00:19:19.461 --> 00:19:21.090
that because we are
the graduate students.

00:19:21.091 --> 00:19:24.330
So, don't take me wrong.
So, I understand that.

00:19:24.331 --> 00:19:25.900
But in the lab like this, we have

00:19:25.901 --> 00:19:29.660
the luxury of doing deployments,

00:19:29.661 --> 00:19:31.895
learning from them,

00:19:31.896 --> 00:19:35.035
and only writing when we
have something to say.

00:19:35.036 --> 00:19:39.525
So, one other thing that
we have learned is,

00:19:39.526 --> 00:19:41.680
as a result of deployments

00:19:41.681 --> 00:19:48.315
in several underserved
communities,

00:19:48.316 --> 00:19:52.820
low resource environments,
how important people

00:19:52.821 --> 00:19:57.425
are in the socio-technical
system that you are designing?

00:19:57.426 --> 00:20:00.350
Where technology,
we repeatedly see,

00:20:00.351 --> 00:20:02.330
takes root only if it is

00:20:02.331 --> 00:20:04.400
an amplifier and it
amplifies people.

00:20:04.401 --> 00:20:05.900
That those are
the only situations

00:20:05.901 --> 00:20:08.340
where technology gets adopted.

00:20:08.460 --> 00:20:10.680
When we see this time and again,

00:20:10.681 --> 00:20:12.410
we see time again when work

00:20:12.411 --> 00:20:14.080
with people with low literacy,

00:20:14.081 --> 00:20:18.315
when we work with people
who are Uber drivers,

00:20:18.316 --> 00:20:21.400
Ola drivers understanding
where they are coming from,

00:20:21.401 --> 00:20:23.010
it's such an important part
of what we do.

00:20:23.011 --> 00:20:25.880
So, we have ethnographers
and so on who just

00:20:25.881 --> 00:20:27.830
immerse themselves
with these communities

00:20:27.831 --> 00:20:29.480
and study what they do,

00:20:29.481 --> 00:20:31.710
even before we even decide
what to do a project.

00:20:31.711 --> 00:20:34.610
So, that's very much in
the culture of the lab.

00:20:34.910 --> 00:20:38.150
Actually, another thing that
I'm very proud about is

00:20:38.151 --> 00:20:39.740
the amount of effort that

00:20:39.741 --> 00:20:41.795
goes into measurement
and evaluation.

00:20:41.796 --> 00:20:44.015
Doing things like
randomized controlled trials

00:20:44.016 --> 00:20:46.550
are now increasingly
using machine learning to

00:20:46.551 --> 00:20:52.740
identify causal
effects and so on.

00:20:53.530 --> 00:20:55.745
But we do write papers,

00:20:55.746 --> 00:20:59.990
and a lot of our work
is also known in

00:20:59.991 --> 00:21:01.760
the academic community for
papers that we've written.

00:21:01.761 --> 00:21:04.080
I want to give an example of,

00:21:04.081 --> 00:21:07.730
I showed you Indrani's
societal impact award.

00:21:07.731 --> 00:21:09.720
That came because of
about 10 years of work

00:21:09.721 --> 00:21:11.835
that she did thinking

00:21:11.836 --> 00:21:15.740
about interface design for
low-literate communities.

00:21:15.741 --> 00:21:17.690
So, that really
presented also a body of

00:21:17.691 --> 00:21:21.495
work that is identified
with that research.

00:21:21.496 --> 00:21:24.510
So Kentaro Toyama, who
started the TM group,

00:21:24.511 --> 00:21:28.205
he also formed
this conference called ICTD,

00:21:28.206 --> 00:21:30.790
which was the first avenue for

00:21:30.791 --> 00:21:34.175
publication of
scholarly work in the space.

00:21:34.176 --> 00:21:36.500
Now, ICTD has its 10th edition.

00:21:36.501 --> 00:21:40.700
It's going to happen in
Ahmedabad next year.

00:21:40.701 --> 00:21:42.480
There are other
conferences like Dev,

00:21:42.481 --> 00:21:44.105
and I think the more recent one
and I think that

00:21:44.106 --> 00:21:45.960
Willy and others have
been also involved in,

00:21:45.961 --> 00:21:46.850
Joyojeet is involved in it,

00:21:46.851 --> 00:21:48.245
I think Pilthi is involved in.

00:21:48.246 --> 00:21:49.965
So, this lab has had

00:21:49.966 --> 00:21:55.210
a significant role in getting
these community started.

00:21:55.211 --> 00:21:59.645
We actively publish in these
communities as well, right?

00:21:59.646 --> 00:22:01.770
I want to mention

00:22:01.771 --> 00:22:04.800
quickly some other things that
we're doing more recently.

00:22:04.801 --> 00:22:06.875
We recently started an effort

00:22:06.876 --> 00:22:09.250
around a societal
impact incubator.

00:22:09.251 --> 00:22:11.530
The reason why we did this was

00:22:11.531 --> 00:22:13.850
to reflect our experiences,

00:22:13.851 --> 00:22:16.860
doing projects like 99DOTS,

00:22:16.861 --> 00:22:18.610
which transitioned into Everwell.

00:22:18.611 --> 00:22:21.805
Digital Green which
transitioned into an NGO.

00:22:21.806 --> 00:22:27.570
So, we have learned
that it's a journey,

00:22:27.571 --> 00:22:29.000
and each journey is different

00:22:29.001 --> 00:22:30.370
in each one of these projects.

00:22:30.371 --> 00:22:32.300
To take something
that we cook up in

00:22:32.301 --> 00:22:34.385
a lab and take it
to the community,

00:22:34.386 --> 00:22:35.925
and not only take the community,

00:22:35.926 --> 00:22:37.910
make it self-sustaining and

00:22:37.911 --> 00:22:39.960
survive outside the lab, right?

00:22:39.961 --> 00:22:43.165
And have an ecosystem
where there's funding,

00:22:43.166 --> 00:22:44.940
there's a business model,

00:22:44.941 --> 00:22:48.475
and there's people that
will take the idea on,

00:22:48.476 --> 00:22:50.410
and the idea just
survives outside the lab.

00:22:50.411 --> 00:22:52.580
We increasingly realize how

00:22:52.581 --> 00:22:56.225
important that aspect of
the research that we do is.

00:22:56.226 --> 00:22:58.850
So, we decided that
we wanted to bring

00:22:58.851 --> 00:23:02.325
in- so, we did this call.

00:23:02.326 --> 00:23:04.570
I'll show you the call
in the next page.

00:23:04.571 --> 00:23:08.900
Where we wanted to bring in
partners, be it government,

00:23:08.901 --> 00:23:10.400
be it social entrepreneurs,

00:23:10.401 --> 00:23:12.280
to work with us to

00:23:12.281 --> 00:23:14.875
participate in that part
of the journey.

00:23:14.876 --> 00:23:16.850
One of the reasons why we did
this was actually we also

00:23:16.851 --> 00:23:18.705
realized that there are
other parts of Microsoft,

00:23:18.706 --> 00:23:19.920
other than Microsoft Research,

00:23:19.921 --> 00:23:21.740
that are very interested
in this activity.

00:23:21.741 --> 00:23:23.570
So, we are partnering
with them to

00:23:23.571 --> 00:23:27.410
do this kind of
societal impact incubator.

00:23:27.411 --> 00:23:29.435
That's the web page.

00:23:29.436 --> 00:23:31.650
We had a call for
collaborative projects on

00:23:31.651 --> 00:23:34.720
Cloud and AI technology
for societal impact.

00:23:34.721 --> 00:23:37.150
These were the themes
that we put out.

00:23:37.151 --> 00:23:38.535
Interestingly, if you see,

00:23:38.536 --> 00:23:40.205
these themes align with

00:23:40.206 --> 00:23:41.805
the projects that I
mentioned to you,

00:23:41.806 --> 00:23:44.610
which is the work that
the people in the lab are doing.

00:23:44.611 --> 00:23:46.405
So, we put this out.

00:23:46.406 --> 00:23:47.320
We thought what's going to

00:23:47.321 --> 00:23:48.505
happen and we received I think,

00:23:48.506 --> 00:23:50.905
I don't know what
the right number is, 150? One?

00:23:50.906 --> 00:23:51.740
&gt;&gt; 175.

00:23:51.741 --> 00:23:52.835
&gt;&gt; 175.

00:23:52.836 --> 00:23:53.890
&gt;&gt; Yes. We thought
maybe we'll get

00:23:53.891 --> 00:23:55.430
50 submissions. We got 175.

00:23:55.431 --> 00:23:56.380
I think now we got to go through

00:23:56.381 --> 00:23:58.005
the process of evaluating them.

00:23:58.006 --> 00:23:59.660
We are going to select like four

00:23:59.661 --> 00:24:00.850
or five or something like that.

00:24:00.851 --> 00:24:02.400
We are going to fund them,

00:24:02.401 --> 00:24:03.950
and every one of them is going to

00:24:03.951 --> 00:24:05.480
have somebody in the lab
to work with them.

00:24:05.481 --> 00:24:06.750
That's actually in
some sense more

00:24:06.751 --> 00:24:08.440
important than the funding
itself that we give.

00:24:08.441 --> 00:24:10.240
We sort of think about this as

00:24:10.241 --> 00:24:12.330
the process of connecting
with the community,

00:24:12.331 --> 00:24:13.680
which is so important
for these projects,

00:24:13.681 --> 00:24:16.800
for them to survive
outside the lab, right?

00:24:16.801 --> 00:24:18.300
Actually that brings into

00:24:18.301 --> 00:24:20.080
point about why we're
doing this workshop.

00:24:20.081 --> 00:24:22.080
The reason why we do
calls like this and

00:24:22.081 --> 00:24:24.875
this workshop is
because we are humble.

00:24:24.876 --> 00:24:27.280
We realize that we cannot
do these things alone.

00:24:27.281 --> 00:24:29.485
The community is such an
important part of what we do.

00:24:29.486 --> 00:24:32.385
That is something that
we've learned over

00:24:32.386 --> 00:24:36.290
the years and that's the reason
why we invite all of you.

00:24:36.440 --> 00:24:39.055
The final topic I'll touch upon

00:24:39.056 --> 00:24:42.545
is social implications of AI.

00:24:42.546 --> 00:24:44.780
This is not true just for AI,

00:24:44.781 --> 00:24:47.955
every time you have new
technology, could be automation,

00:24:47.956 --> 00:24:50.820
could be nuclear power,

00:24:51.130 --> 00:24:53.765
technology can cut both ways.

00:24:53.766 --> 00:24:55.765
It can do good, it can do harm.

00:24:55.766 --> 00:24:58.380
Any discussion about technology,

00:24:58.381 --> 00:24:59.825
and particularly I think AI,

00:24:59.826 --> 00:25:01.750
has to actually consider

00:25:01.751 --> 00:25:05.650
social ethical implications
of what we're doing.

00:25:05.651 --> 00:25:08.800
So, Jackie O'Neill
pointed this page to me.

00:25:08.801 --> 00:25:10.240
This is a very interesting report

00:25:10.241 --> 00:25:13.130
that if you haven't read
I encourage reading it.

00:25:13.131 --> 00:25:17.960
It's a institute in
NYU called AINOW.

00:25:17.961 --> 00:25:20.750
They have a report that
is AINOW 2016 report

00:25:20.751 --> 00:25:23.440
and there's also
an AINOW 2017 report.

00:25:23.441 --> 00:25:27.450
It brings out issues
about labor and

00:25:27.451 --> 00:25:29.695
automation and potential job loss

00:25:29.696 --> 00:25:31.850
from AI. How to think about that?

00:25:31.851 --> 00:25:33.960
How to think about
bias and inclusion?

00:25:33.961 --> 00:25:37.670
Which is fairness,
which is very important

00:25:37.671 --> 00:25:39.680
when we use training data

00:25:39.681 --> 00:25:41.550
to train machine
learning algorithms.

00:25:41.551 --> 00:25:43.615
Whether we are training them

00:25:43.616 --> 00:25:46.150
with a representative data
set that represents all of

00:25:46.151 --> 00:25:48.760
us rather than omit

00:25:48.761 --> 00:25:50.270
certain segments
of the population,

00:25:50.271 --> 00:25:52.670
rights, and liberties.

00:25:52.671 --> 00:25:55.630
Particularly about the
use of AI technology

00:25:55.631 --> 00:25:58.540
by government around surveillance
and video surveillance,

00:25:58.541 --> 00:26:00.990
facial recognition,
issues like that,

00:26:00.991 --> 00:26:02.235
and more broadly around

00:26:02.236 --> 00:26:03.895
issues around ethics
and governance.

00:26:03.896 --> 00:26:06.440
I think that's such an
important topic to discuss.

00:26:06.441 --> 00:26:07.900
I hope that when you have

00:26:07.901 --> 00:26:09.220
your breakout sessions in

00:26:09.221 --> 00:26:10.605
addition to discussing
technology,

00:26:10.606 --> 00:26:12.110
I hope that we will also discuss

00:26:12.111 --> 00:26:15.775
these topics integrally
of part of what we we do.

00:26:15.776 --> 00:26:20.900
Interestingly, we have
inside Microsoft,

00:26:20.901 --> 00:26:23.850
my manager, his name
is Eric Horvitz.

00:26:23.851 --> 00:26:25.390
He is a big proponent of

00:26:25.391 --> 00:26:28.020
careful consideration of
AI and ethics in society.

00:26:28.021 --> 00:26:30.620
He has formed a committee
called the Aether Committee,

00:26:30.621 --> 00:26:33.890
which stands for AI,

00:26:33.891 --> 00:26:36.080
Ethics and Effects in
Engineering and Research.

00:26:36.081 --> 00:26:37.730
These are the subcommittees,

00:26:37.731 --> 00:26:39.090
there are seven
working groups that

00:26:39.091 --> 00:26:40.660
are actively working inside

00:26:40.661 --> 00:26:44.095
Microsoft Research and Microsoft,

00:26:44.096 --> 00:26:46.080
around all these various
topics ranging from

00:26:46.081 --> 00:26:48.185
sensitive uses,
bias and fairness.

00:26:48.186 --> 00:26:50.210
Interestingly,
there's a coverage of

00:26:50.211 --> 00:26:52.050
these seven topics
among the five things

00:26:52.051 --> 00:26:56.605
that you saw in the AINOW report.

00:26:56.606 --> 00:27:02.125
For example, I think AJ
Kumar, that Willy knows,

00:27:02.126 --> 00:27:04.955
I think she's a co-head

00:27:04.956 --> 00:27:08.240
of the Group on bias and
fairness for example.

00:27:08.241 --> 00:27:11.995
So, we have resources
even inside Microsoft

00:27:11.996 --> 00:27:16.305
to bring to bare on
these kinds of conversations,

00:27:16.306 --> 00:27:18.030
and I encourage all of
you to make use of all of

00:27:18.031 --> 00:27:20.315
these resources in
this conversation.

00:27:20.316 --> 00:27:23.725
So, I'm almost about to end.

